-- title:
--   Algebra generated by the representatives of a representation
-- statement:
--   A representation $U$ of a group $G$ on $H$ generates the algebra of linear combinations of the representatives $\{U_g : g\in G\}$, often called a *group algebra*. Since the representatives contain the identity and are closed under products, this is the subalgebra of the operators on $H$ generated by them. The commutant of a representation, $\mathcal C(U)$, is the commutant of this algebra.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The definition uses `Algebra.adjoin`.
-- source:
--   Ragone et al., Representation Theory for Geometric Quantum Machine Learning, arXiv:2210.07980v2, p.28, the sentence before Definition 17

import Mathlib

namespace RepGQML

/-- The algebra generated by the representatives `{U_g : g ∈ G}` of a representation: "unitary
representations `U` of `G` generate algebras `A` by allowing linear combinations of the
representatives `{U_g : g ∈ G}` (this is often called a *group algebra*)" (Ragone et al., *Representation Theory for Geometric Quantum Machine Learning*, arXiv:2210.07980v2, p.28).

**Formalization Note** The representatives are closed under products and contain the identity, so
their linear span is the subalgebra they generate, which is what `Algebra.adjoin` gives. -/
def groupAlgebra {k G H : Type*} [Field k] [Group G] [AddCommGroup H] [Module k H]
    (U : Representation k G H) : Subalgebra k (Module.End k H) :=
  Algebra.adjoin k (Set.range U)

end RepGQML


